# A subject of two letters that are not ASCII and 46 blanks.  The blanks do
# not fit on the line.  The first is written as a plain blank after the
# encoded letters, the other 45 as encoded words of '_', 32 on the first
# line and 13 on the second.  A reader drops a plain blank between two
# encoded words (RFC 2047, 6.2), so it decodes 45 blanks where the subject
# has 46.  (With 45 blanks they all fit on the first line, and none is
# lost.)  The expected file records what the library writes today, not
# what it should write.
mode=accept
tls=none
form=(From=alice@example.com To=bob@example.net Body=$'Hi\n' User=alice Password=secret
      Subject="$(printf '\xc3\xa9\xc3\xa9')$(printf ' %.0s' $(seq 46))")
